WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.241
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.241 --> 00:00:08.934
I have a confession to make.

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:10.908
I have an addiction.

00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:14.580
My addiction is so compulsive,
it interferes with everyday life.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.280
I'm not as productive as I want to be.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.074
I often find it hard to focus,
and when people tell me to stop,

00:00:23.074 --> 00:00:24.838
I simply ignore them.

00:00:24.838 --> 00:00:27.946
I'm addicted to my smartphone.

00:00:27.946 --> 00:00:32.452
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Really I'm addicted to distraction, and

00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:34.200
many of us here are too.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.910
On a regular day, we unlock our cell
phones more than 120 times per day.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.320
I decided to run an experiment to
better understand that addiction.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.510
I, of course, used my phone to
download an app called Moment and

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:50.680
began to track my usage.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:56.810
I apparently unlock my cell phone
more than 130 times per day.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.570
That is every nine minutes.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.360
What is so gripping that I can't resist?

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.038
On a good day,

00:01:03.038 --> 00:01:06.444
I'm stalking people on Facebook I
haven't seen since middle school.

00:01:06.444 --> 00:01:09.236
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And on a bad day,

00:01:09.236 --> 00:01:13.805
I'm reading listicles, like ten cute
child stars who grew up to be ugly.

00:01:13.805 --> 00:01:18.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Why do so

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:23.060
many of us feel this compulsive
urge to grab our phone?

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.470
Because our phone is like
a slot machine in Vegas.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:31.419
We never know what reward, new tweet, new
email, new text message, that child star

00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:36.180
turned ugly, awaits us, and the result,
120 times per day we lose our focus.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.880
Don't get me wrong here,
I love technology,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.675
because technology gives us superpowers.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.145
Superpowers to see my boyfriend
in Berlin every night and

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:51.796
to obtain information about
anything in an instant,

00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:55.550
or to get instant memories
about thoughts and images.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:00.641
So sometimes you do use these superpowers
wisely, but most often we don't.

00:02:00.641 --> 00:02:01.590
And the result?

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.210
120 times per day we lose our focus.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.770
There's a tremendous cost to this,
at work, in our relationships,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.600
and with ourselves.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:19.910
At work, we could use these superpowers
to do research or to talk to colleagues.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.450
Instead, we're addicted to distraction.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.320
Look at me.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.590
On a typical day, I'm sitting on
my computer doing actual work and

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.660
quickly check my phone, see my messages,
take a phone call, check my emails on

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.575
my phone to then immediately hit
Refresh on my computer emails again.

00:02:35.575 --> 00:02:39.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; On days like this, I could have as well

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.873
stared at the wall for
two hours because according to research,

00:02:42.873 --> 00:02:47.094
my productively levels are the same
as after pulling an all-nighter.

00:02:47.094 --> 00:02:51.490
At work, I'm present, but
absent, and maybe you are too.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:57.610
In our relationships, we could use
our superpowers to talk to family and

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.680
friends all over the world, instead,
we're often present but absent.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.350
A friend of mine recently said lately,
my fiance and I come home and grab dinner.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.277
We are on our email still, and

00:03:11.277 --> 00:03:16.090
that makes us feel we're together,
but alone.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.857
Sometimes I even reject his advances,

00:03:20.857 --> 00:03:25.441
because I quickly want to finish
looking through my Facebook friends.

00:03:25.441 --> 00:03:30.175
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We've gone now from stonewalling to

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:31.440
phone walling.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:38.760
As the relationship expert, John Gottman
found, in our relationship, the number

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.590
one predictor of an unhappy union,
is a lack of responsiveness and attention.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.311
In her relationship, my friend often
finds herself present, but absent.

00:03:49.311 --> 00:03:52.027
And maybe you do, too.

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:53.950
Let's look at ourselves.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.430
When we're along with our thoughts, we
could use our superpowers to capture our

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.330
thoughts or
read interesting books and articles.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.916
Instead we're addicted to distraction.

00:04:07.916 --> 00:04:12.080
What would you prefer, pain or boredom?

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.670
And in an experiment,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.670
researchers gave participants a small
electric shock that was so unpleasant

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.940
that three-quarters of them were willing
to pay not to experience that shock again.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:28.700
Then research put those same participants
into a room to sit alone for 15 minutes.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:33.527
Subjects were so uncomfortable being
alone with their own thoughts that they

00:04:33.527 --> 00:04:35.445
now shocked themselves voluntarily.

00:04:35.445 --> 00:04:40.147
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; One outlier pressed that button

00:04:40.147 --> 00:04:42.370
a 190 times in 15 minutes.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.532
Shocking, I know.

00:04:44.532 --> 00:04:49.758
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; These participants when they're

00:04:49.758 --> 00:04:56.040
alone are absent but present but absent.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.860
And maybe you are, too.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.700
So this is the world we live in.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.740
Today, I want to sketch
out a new world for you.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.460
A world where we can finally
make use of these superpowers

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.650
to live a more present life at work,
in our relationships, and with ourself.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:16.350
At work, there is a concept
which is called deep work

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.960
which makes you focus without
distraction on a single task.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.070
And the result, increased productivity,
improved performance, and

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.610
the sense of true fulfillment
that comes from craftsmanship.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.690
I tried it.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.530
When writing this talk, I went and
blocked four hour slots.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.230
I'll use my phone for research but ignored
all notifications, emails, and texts.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:38.470
The result?

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:43.480
I felt productive in a way I had
not felt in a very long time.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.910
I felt satisfied with my work and
with myself.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:49.440
I felt effective, and maybe you would too.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.160
Let's look at our relationships.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.130
My mother helped me see a different
world during Christmas.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.390
Her simple recipe was a cell phone bowl.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:03.520
During meals and family time, we had
to put our cell phone into that bowl.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.653
Today, I'm grateful for
the world she created for us.

00:06:07.653 --> 00:06:13.375
Christmas truly was endless cookie smells,
laughter until we had tears in our eyes.

00:06:13.375 --> 00:06:17.635
And my grandmother who came grey and
looking much older than I remember her

00:06:17.635 --> 00:06:24.765
even looking rejuvenated with red
cheeks and a beautiful smirk.

00:06:24.765 --> 00:06:29.300
During that time, I felt pure joy,
and maybe you would, too.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.250
Let's look at ourselves.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.770
After that memorable Christmas, I was
sitting on a plane back to San Francisco.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:43.270
I was sitting in my seat with no
emails and texts to distract myself.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.430
And my mind went to how excited I
felt flying back into Silicon Valley,

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.430
that this is what Florence must
have felt like in the Renaissance.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:58.497
I then started wondering about my
constant struggle to juggle my life in

00:06:58.497 --> 00:07:00.257
Germany with my life here.

00:07:00.257 --> 00:07:03.662
For more than a year,
I've been debating whether to stay here or

00:07:03.662 --> 00:07:05.330
to go home after graduation.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:11.240
I started thinking about how grateful
I was for this wonderful family,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.525
and I wondered if they knew how much
this time with them meant to me.

00:07:14.525 --> 00:07:19.526
Silent tears started running down my face,
and in that moment,

00:07:19.526 --> 00:07:23.786
I decided that in spite of
all the opportunities here,

00:07:23.786 --> 00:07:28.248
I will go home after graduation
because it simply felt right.

00:07:28.248 --> 00:07:34.151
Landing in San Francisco that morning,
I felt deeply in touch with myself.

00:07:34.151 --> 00:07:37.583
What about a world where we don't
feel FOMO because of social media,

00:07:37.583 --> 00:07:41.380
but because we haven't taken the time
to catch up with our mental news feed.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:47.350
After that time in that plane,
I felt clear and grounded,

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.130
and maybe you would too.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.850
So how can you all deal with your
own addiction to distraction?

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:01.310
What worked for
me is to take present time and space for

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:05.090
work, to take time,
focused time with my family,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.210
and to take that time and
space to be alone with my own thoughts.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:13.870
Today, I'm finally using those superpowers
that technology gives me to live a more

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.320
present life at work,
in my relationships, and with myself.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.330
I invite you to do the same.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.390
Take focused time and
space where distraction is not an option,

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:29.977
where work is only work, where
conversation is only conversation, and

00:08:29.977 --> 00:08:35.265
where self-reflection enables you to
expand more than you thought was possible.

00:08:35.265 --> 00:08:38.071
In the philosopher, Eckhart Tolle's words,

00:08:38.071 --> 00:08:42.924
it is the quality of our consciousness at
a given moment, that shapes our future.

00:08:42.924 --> 00:08:45.803
What do you want the future to be for you?

00:08:45.803 --> 00:08:46.825
Thank you.

00:08:46.825 --> 00:08:49.890
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.629
[MUSIC]

